\title{Base types}
\import{prelude}

\p{Coln includes two base types for demonstration purposes:
\code{\kwi{String}} and \code{\kwi{Int}}. An element of \code{\kwi{String}} is a single
string and an element of \code{\kwi{Int}} is a single (machine) integer.
Both are ultimately stored as a single database column. As we saw
previously, we can use one of these types as a very simple schema:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} FavouriteNumber := \kwi{Int}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : \kwi{Int}}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{19}}
  }

\p{\strong{Constraints:}
\ul{
  \li{\code{root} has exactly one row.}
}
}
}

\p{This is the schema of a \em{single} \code{\kwi{Int}}, rather than the schema of a table of \code{\kwi{Int}}s. Not so useful by itself, but we will soon be forming more complicated theories out of simple pieces like this.}

\collapsedaside{A word of caution: the user is not currently prevented from writing theories that would require an infinite amount of storage to produce a valid instance of, for example, the theory of [functions](001E) \code{\kwi{String} -> \kwi{String}}.}

% \p{The solution to this problem requires building up a little more theory, so we do not address it right now, and instead continue on to give a full account of the “purely combinatorial” fragment of Coln. \mvrnote{Has the fix here actually been figured out?}}
